\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exist three distinct lines  \n\\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) covering all points of \\(P\\) and that exactly two of them are sunny.  \nLet the unique non‑sunny line be denoted by \\(N\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Geometry of the non‑sunny line \\(N\\)\n\nA non‑sunny line is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c .\n\\]\n\nFrom the coordinates of the points in \\(P\\) we obtain the following possibilities:\n\n| type of \\(N\\) | values of the constant giving at least two points of \\(P\\) | maximal number of points of \\(P\\) on \\(N\\) |\n|---------------|------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|\n| \\(x=c\\)       | \\(c=1,2,3\\)                                                | \\(c=1\\) contains three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\); \\(c=2\\) contains two points \\((2,1),(2,2)\\); \\(c=3\\) contains one point \\((3,1)\\). |\n| \\(y=c\\)       | \\(c=1,2,3\\)                                                | \\(c=1\\) contains three points \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\); \\(c=2\\) contains two points \\((1,2),(2,2)\\); \\(c=3\\) contains one point \\((1,3)\\). |\n| \\(x+y=c\\)     | \\(c=2,3,4\\)                                                | \\(c=2\\) contains one point \\((1,1)\\); \\(c=3\\) contains two points \\((1,2),(2,1)\\); \\(c=4\\) contains three points \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\). |\n\nThus \\(N\\) can contain at most three points of \\(P\\); the only cases with three points are  \n\n\\[\nN\\in\\{x=1,\\; y=1,\\; x+y=4\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points that must be covered by the two sunny lines\n\nLet \\(R=P\\setminus N\\) be the set of points not lying on the non‑sunny line.\nWe treat the three possibilities for \\(N\\) separately.\n\n---\n\n#### Case A: \\(N:x=1\\)\n\n\\(N\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(2,1),(3,1),(2,2)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny line through two points of \\(R\\) has one of the following slopes:\n\n* \\((2,1)-(3,1)\\): slope \\(0\\) (horizontal, non‑sunny);\n* \\((2,1)-(2,2)\\): slope \\(\\infty\\) (vertical, non‑sunny);\n* \\((3,1)-(2,2)\\): slope \\(-1\\) (non‑sunny).\n\nConsequently **no sunny line can contain two points of \\(R\\)**; each sunny line can cover at most one point of \\(R\\).  \nSince \\(|R|=3\\), two sunny lines cannot cover all of \\(R\\). Contradiction.\n\n---\n\n#### Case B: \\(N:y=1\\)\n\n\\(N\\) contains \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe slopes of the three possible pairs are respectively \\(\\infty\\), \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\), all non‑sunny.  \nThus again each sunny line can cover at most one point of \\(R\\), but \\(|R|=3\\). Contradiction.\n\n---\n\n#### Case C: \\(N:x+y=4\\)\n\n\\(N\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe slopes of the three possible pairs are \\(0\\) (between \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\)), \\(\\infty\\) (between \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\)), and \\(-1\\) (between \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\)).  \nAll are non‑sunny, so each sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(R\\).  \nSince \\(|R|=3\\), two sunny lines are insufficient. Contradiction.\n\n---\n\n#### Remaining possibilities for \\(N\\)\n\nIf \\(N\\) is any of the other non‑sunny lines (\\(x=2\\), \\(y=2\\), \\(x+y=3\\)), then \\(|N\\cap P|=2\\) and consequently \\(|R|=4\\).  \nA direct inspection of the four points in each such \\(R\\) shows that **any two of them share either the same \\(x\\), the same \\(y\\), or the same sum \\(x+y\\)**; therefore any line through two of them has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) and is non‑sunny. Hence each sunny line can cover at most one point of \\(R\\), and with \\(|R|=4\\) two sunny lines cannot suffice.  \n\nThus every possible shape of the non‑sunny line leads to a contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nNo arrangement of three distinct lines can cover all six points of \\(P\\) while having exactly two sunny lines. Therefore for \\(n=3\\) the value \\(k=2\\) is **impossible**.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{ there is no configuration with }k=2.}\n\\]"
