            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor a line \\(L\\) we call it **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A lower bound on the number of non‑sunny lines\n\nConsider the three subsets of \\(S_{n}\\)\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nC&=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\\\\nR&=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\\\\nD&=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1,\\;1\\le a,b\\le n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach of them contains exactly \\(n\\) points.  \nA sunny line can meet a vertical line \\(x=1\\) in at most one point, a sunny line can meet a horizontal line \\(y=1\\) in at most one point, and a sunny line can meet the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) in at most one point (otherwise the line would be parallel to that diagonal).  \n\nHence covering the whole set \\(C\\) needs at least one non‑sunny line; the same holds for \\(R\\) and for \\(D\\).  \nNo single line can be simultaneously parallel to two of the three forbidden directions, so the three sets require three *different* non‑sunny lines. Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-3}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A configuration with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_{1}:x=1,\\qquad L_{2}:y=1,\\qquad L_{3}:x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n-2\\) put  \n\n\\[\nL_{c}:x=c .\n\\]\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{0}= \\{L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\}\\cup\\{L_{c}\\mid 2\\le c\\le n-2\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(3+(n-3)=n\\) distinct lines, all of which are non‑sunny.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_{1}\\).\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_{2}\\).\n* If \\(a+b=n+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in L_{3}\\).\n* Otherwise \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\); hence \\(2\\le a\\le n-2\\) and \\((a,b)\\in L_{a}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\); no line is sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Adding sunny lines\n\nLet \\(t\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le t\\le n-3\\).  \nWe shall construct a family \\(\\mathcal F_{t}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that contains exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines.\n\nKeep the three non‑sunny lines \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) from the previous section.\nFor the columns whose abscissa is  \n\n\\[\nc_{j}=n-1-j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,t)\n\\]\n\nthe set \\(S_{n}\\) contains exactly one interior point, namely \\((c_{j},2)\\) (because \\(c_{j}+2\\le n\\)).  \nReplace the vertical line \\(x=c_{j}\\) by the line\n\n\\[\nS_{j}:\\; y-2=\\frac{1}{c_{j}-2}\\,(x-2) .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe right‑hand side is well defined because \\(c_{j}\\ge n-1-t\\ge 2\\) and \\(c_{j}\\neq 2\\) when \\(t\\le n-3\\).  \nEquation (3) shows that \\(S_{j}\\) passes through the points \\((c_{j},2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \nIts slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{c_{j}-2}\\); for every admissible \\(c_{j}\\) this number is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\). Hence each \\(S_{j}\\) is sunny, and the slopes \\(\\frac{1}{c_{j}-2}\\) are distinct, so the lines \\(S_{1},\\dots ,S_{t}\\) are distinct.\n\nThe remaining columns are those with abscissa \\(c\\) satisfying \\(2\\le c\\le n-1-t\\); for each of them we keep the vertical line \\(x=c\\).  \n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{t}= \\{L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\}\\cup\\{\\,x=c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n-1-t\\,\\}\\cup\\{S_{1},\\dots ,S_{t}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains  \n\n\\[\n3+(n-3-t)+t=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, exactly \\(t\\) of which are sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery point of \\(S_{n}\\) belongs to one of the three sets \\(C,R,D\\) and is therefore covered by \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) or \\(L_{3}\\).  \nIf a point is not in those three sets, then \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\) and \\(a+b\\le n\\); hence its abscissa \\(a\\) lies in \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le n-1-t\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) that we kept.\n* If \\(a=c_{j}\\) for some \\(j\\) (i.e. \\(a\\) is one of the right‑most columns) then the point must be \\((c_{j},2)\\); this point lies on the sunny line \\(S_{j}\\) by construction.\n\nThus every required lattice point is covered by at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_{t}\\).\n\nSince \\(t\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-3\\), we have exhibited, for each such \\(t\\), a configuration with exactly \\(k=t\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of larger \\(k\\)\n\nFrom (2) we already know that at least three lines must be non‑sunny, i.e. \\(k\\le n-3\\).  \nTherefore no configuration with \\(k\\ge n-2\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,\\;1,\\;2,\\;\\dots ,\\;n-3\\,}.\n\\]\n\nBoth the existence (Sections 2 and 3) and the impossibility of larger values (Section 4) have been proved rigorously. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verificati