he equality would require \\(i+\\sigma(i)=2\\), impossible for \\(i,\\sigma(i)\\ge2\\)).  \n   Therefore **all** lines \\(L_i\\;(i\\ge2)\\) are sunny.  \n   The only line whose \u201csunny\u2011ness\u2019\u2019 is not yet decided is \\(L_1\\).  \n   Consequently the *largest* possible number of sunny lines equals \\(n\\) (if we choose \\(L_1\\) sunny) and the *smallest* possible number equals \\(0\\) (if we choose every line non\u2011sunny).  \n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le n\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   we shall construct a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Step\u202f1 \u2013 a derangement.*  \n   Fix any derangement \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n   (For \\(n\\ge3\\) such a derangement always exists; for \\(n=3\\) we may take \\(\\sigma(2)=3,\\;\\sigma(3)=2\\).)\n\n   *Step\u202f2 \u2013 the \\(n-1\\) lines meeting both edges.*  \n   For each \\(i=2,\\dots ,n\\) define the line \\(L_i\\) as in (1).  \n   All these lines are sunny, as observed above.\n\n   *Step\u202f3 \u2013 the remaining line through \\((1,1)\\).*  \n   Choose a line \\(L_1\\) through \\((1,1)\\) as follows.\n\n   * If we want \\(L_1\\) to be sunny, we take the line \\(y=x\\) (slope\u202f\\(1\\)).  \n   * If we want \\(L_1\\) to be non\u2011sunny, we take the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\n\n   In both cases \\(L_1\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and possibly other points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Step\u202f4 \u2013 verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines.*  \n\n   \u2013 **Points on the two edges.**  \n   By construction, each point \\((1,i)\\;(i\\ge2)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\) and each point \\((i,1)\\;(i\\ge2)\\) lies on the same line \\(L_i\\).  \n   The point \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(L_1\\).\n\n   \u2013 **Interior points.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\).  \n   Set \\(i=b\\) (so \\(2\\le i\\le n\\)).  \n   Because \\(\\sigma\\