            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad N_n=|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the three directions\n\\(\\ell_x:\\) \\(x\\)-axis, \\(\\ell_y:\\) \\(y\\)-axis, \\(\\ell_d:\\) \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.   Edge points force distinct lines\n\nThe points  \n\n\\[\nE_x=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad\nE_y=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nlie on the two edges of the triangle.  \nIf a line contained two distinct points of \\(E_x\\) it would be vertical, i.e. parallel to \\(\\ell_y\\); similarly a line containing two points of \\(E_y\\) would be horizontal, i.e. parallel to \\(\\ell_x\\).  \nHence **each point of \\(E_x\\cup E_y\\) must belong to a different line**.  \nBecause \\(|E_x\\cup E_y|=2n-1\\) and we are allowed exactly \\(n\\) lines, every admissible family of \\(n\\) lines contains precisely one line through each point of \\(E_x\\) and each point of \\(E_y\\); the point \\((1,1)\\) belongs to the same line as both edges.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.   A convenient parametrisation\n\nChoose a permutation  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nwith **no fixed point** (\\(\\sigma(i)\\neq i\\) for all \\(i\\)).  \nSuch a *derangement* exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\) (for \\(n=3\\) we may take \\(\\sigma(2)=3,\\;\\sigma(3)=2\\)).\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i\\;:\\;(1,i)\\text{ and }(\\sigma(i),1).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause the two defining points have different abscissae and different ordinates, \\(L_i\\) is neither vertical nor horizontal.  \nIts slope equals \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-i}{\\sigma(i)-1}\\).  \nIf this slope were \\(-1\\) we would have \\(1-i=-(\\sigma(i)-1)\\), i.e. \\(i+\\sigma(i)=2\\), impossible for \\(i,\\sigma(i)\\ge2\\).  \nThus **every line \\(L_i\\) is sunny**.\n\nFinally let  \n\n\\[\nL_1\\;:\\;(1,1)\\text{ together with an arbitrary direction}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe direction of \\(L_1\\) will be chosen later in order to obtain the required number of sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.   The family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covers all points of \\(T_n\\)\n\n*Edge points.*  \nFor \\(i\\ge2\\) the line \\(L_i\\) contains the two edge points \\((1,i)\\in E_x\\) and \\((\\sigma(i),1)\\in E_y\\).  \nThe point \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(L_1\\).  \nHence every point of \\(E_x\\cup E_y\\) is covered.\n\n*Interior points.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\).  \nSet \\(i=b\\) (so \\(2\\le i\\le n\\)).  \nBecause \\(\\sigma\\) is a bijection, there exists a unique index \\(j\\) such that \\(\\sigma(j)=a\\).  \nConsider the line \\(L_j\\) defined in (5).  \nIts equation can be written as  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{y-j}{x-1}= \\frac{1-j}{\\sigma(j)-1}= \\frac{1-j}{a-1}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\((x,y)=(a,b)\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b-j}{a-1}= \\frac{1-j}{a-1}\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;b=j .\n\\]\n\nSince we chose \\(j=b\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (7) and therefore belongs to \\(L_j\\).  \nThus **every interior point of the triangle lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_2,\\dots ,L_n\\)**.\n\nConsequently the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,L_2,\\dots ,L_n\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers the whole \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.   Controlling the number of sunny lines\n\nAll lines \\(L_i\\;(i\\ge2)\\) are sunny by construction.  \nThe line \\(L_1\\) can be made sunny (choose \\(L_1\\) to be \\(y=x\\)) or non‑sunny (choose \\(L_1\\) to be the vertical line \\(x=1\\)).  \nHence the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) yields configurations with **\\(n\\)** sunny lines (all sunny) and with **\\(n-1\\)** sunny lines (only \\(L_1\\) non‑sunny).\n\nTo obtain configurations with fewer sunny lines we replace some of the sunny lines \\(L_i\\;(i\\ge2)\\) by a non‑sunny line that still passes through the same two edge points.  \nFor a fixed \\(i\\ge2\\) the three non‑sunny directions that contain the points \\((1,i)\\) and \\((\\sigma(i),1)\\) are:\n\n* the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (parallel to \\(\\ell_y\\));\n* the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (parallel to \\(\\ell_x\\));\n* the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\)).\n\nEach of these three lines contains the two required edge points, and, as shown in Section 3, the interior points are already covered by the *other* lines of the family, so the replacement does not destroy the covering property.\n\nBecause the choice for each index \\(i\\ge2\\) is independent, we may turn **any** subset of the lines \\(\\{L_2,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) into non‑sunny lines.  \nIf we turn exactly \\(t\\) of them non‑sunny and keep the remaining \\(n-1-t\\) sunny, and we also decide whether \\(L_1\\) is sunny or not, the total number of sunny lines becomes  \n\n\\[\nk=\n\\begin{cases}\nn-t      &\\text{if }L_1\\text{ is sunny},\\\\[1mm]\nn-1-t    &\\text{if }L_1\\text{ is non‑sunny}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-1\\), the set of attainable values for \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.   Final answer\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely all non‑negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, for \\(n=3\\) the possible values are \\(k=0,1,2,3\\); for any larger \\(n\\) every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) occurs. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
