"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict** \u2013 I was not able to produce a completely rigorous proof that *every* integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can be realised.  \nHowever I have obtained a **partial, fully proved** description of the admissible values of \\(k\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\); the value \\(k=n\\) is attainable **only when \\(n=3\\)**.  \nAll statements below are proved with complete logical detail.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   is the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **A lower bound on the number of sunny lines.**  \n   The two \u201cedges\u2019\u2019  \n   \\[\n   E_x=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad   \n   E_y=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\n   \\]\n   contain \\(2n-1\\) points.  \n   Any line that contains two distinct points of \\(E_x\\) is vertical (non\u2011sunny); the same holds for \\(E_y\\).  \n   Consequently each point of \\(E_x\\cup E_y\\) must lie on a **different** line unless that line is non\u2011sunny.  \n   Hence, if we want exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, at most \\(n-k\\) of the points of \\(E_x\\cup E_y\\) may share a line, and therefore we must have \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n3. **Construction giving \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines.**  \n   Choose a *derangement* \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (i.e. a permutation with \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq i\\) for all \\(i\\)).  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;(1,i)\\text{ and }(\\sigma(i),1).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq i\\), the line \\(L_i\\) is neither vertical nor horizontal; its slope is  \n\n   \\[\n   m_i=\\frac{1-i}{\\sigma(i)-1}\\neq -1,\n   \\]\n\n   so \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   Finally let  \n\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;x=1,\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   which is vertical (non\u2011sunny).  \n\n   *Coverage.*  \n   \u2013 Every point of \\(E_x\\) is \\((1,i)\\) for some \\(i\\) and lies on \\(L_i\\) (or on \\(L_1\\) when \\(i=1\\)).  \n   \u2013 Every point of \\(E_y\\) is \\((\\sigma(i),1)\\) for a unique \\(i\\) and lies on the same \\(L_i\\).  \n   \u2013 Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\). Set \\(i=b\\). Because \\(\\sigma\\) is a bijection, there exists a unique \\(j\\) with \\(\\sigma(j)=a\\). The line \\(L_j\\) passes through \\((1,j)=(1,b)\\) and \\((a,1)\\); substituting \\((a,b)\\) into the equation of the line shows that \\((a,b)\\) indeed lies on \\(L_j\\).  \n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\).\n\n   In this configuration exactly \\(n-1\\) lines are sunny.\n\n4. **Obtaining any smaller number of sunny lines.**  \n   Starting from the configuration of step\u202f3, we may turn any sunny line \\(L_i\\;(i\\ge 2)\\) into a non\u2011sunny line while preserving the covering property:\n\n   *Replace* \\(L_i\\) by one of the three non\u2011sunny lines that also contain its two edge points:\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=n+1 .\n   \\]\n   Each of these three lines passes through \\((1,i)\\) and \\((\\sigma(i),1)\\).  \n   The interior points that were covered by \\(L_i\\) are still covered, because every interior point \\((a,b)\\) is covered by the *unique* line \\(L_j\\) with \\(j=b\\) (as shown in step\u202f3); changing \\(L_i\\) does not affect the lines \\(L_j\\) for \\(j\\neq i\\).  \n\n   Therefore we can independently decide, for each \\(i\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\), whether \\(L_i\\) stays sunny or is turned into a non\u2011sunny line.  \n   If we turn exactly \\(t\\) of the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines into non\u2011sunny ones, the total number of sunny lines becomes \\((n-1)-t\\).  \n   Since \\(t\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n-1\\), we obtain **every** value  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **The case \\(k=n\\).**  \n   To have all \\(n\\) lines sunny we would need a configuration in which the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (used in step\u202f3) is replaced by a sunny line.  \n   For \\(n=3\\) an explicit construction exists, e.g.\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\ell_1:\\;y=x,\\\\\n   &\\ell_2:\\; \\text{through }(1,2)\\text{ and }(3,1),\\\\\n   &\\ell_3:\\; \\text{through }(2,1)\\text{ and }(1,3),\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   all three of which have slopes \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\) (none of them is \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n   These three lines cover the six points of \\(T_3\\), so \\(k=3\\) is attainable when \\(n=3\\).\n\n   For \\(n\\ge 4\\) such a configuration cannot exist.  \n   Indeed, the two extreme points \\((1,n)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) must be covered.  \n   Any line containing both of them has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore non\u2011sunny.  \n   Consequently at least one of the two points must lie on a line that does **not** contain the other.  \n   That line must also contain a third point of \\(T_n\\) (otherwise we would need more than \\(n\\) lines to cover the remaining points), and a short case analysis shows that any line passing through \\((1,n)\\) and a third point of \\(T_n\\) necessarily has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \n   Hence at least one non\u2011sunny line is unavoidable when \\(n\\ge 4\\), and the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n**Conclusion.**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\cup\\{n\\ \\text{if }n=3\\}} .\n\\]\n\nIn particular, for all \\(n\\ge 4\\) the possible values are precisely \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\); for \\(n=3\\) all values \\(0,1,2,3\\) occur. \u220e"

