            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a completely rigorous construction for *every* admissible value of \\(k\\).  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the following:\n\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is **impossible** when \\(n\\ge 4\\); the only case in which \\(k=n\\) can occur is \\(n=3\\).\n* The value \\(k=0\\) is always attainable (take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n\\)).\n* The value \\(k=n-1\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) (a concrete construction is given below).\n\nFrom the construction for \\(k=n-1\\) one can turn any sunny line into a non‑sunny line **without destroying the covering property**, thereby obtaining configurations with any smaller number of sunny lines. Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\cup\\{n\\ \\text{if }n=3\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThe proof of the impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\) and the constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n-1\\) are given in full detail below.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n   is the set of lattice points that must be covered.  \n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound.**  \n   Because we are allowed to use only \\(n\\) distinct lines, trivially \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n   *Every sunny line can contain at most one point of the set*  \n   \\[\n   C=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n   R=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\n   \\]\n   because two distinct points of \\(C\\) (resp. \\(R\\)) would force the line to be vertical (resp. horizontal).  \n   Moreover a sunny line can contain at most one point of the anti‑diagonal  \n   \\[\n   D=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\},\n   \\]\n   because two points of \\(D\\) would give slope \\(-1\\).  \n\n   The point \\((1,1)\\) belongs to all three families \\(C,R,D\\).  \n   If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, each of the \\(n\\) points of \\(C\\) would have to lie on a different sunny line, and the same would be true for the \\(n\\) points of \\(R\\).  \n   Hence the line that contains \\((1,1)\\) would have to be the *only* line that contains a point of \\(C\\) **and** a point of \\(R\\).  \n   Consequently that line could not contain any other point of \\(R\\); therefore some other line would have to contain two distinct points of \\(R\\), which would make it horizontal – a non‑sunny line.  \n   This contradiction shows that at least one of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny when \\(n\\ge4\\); i.e. \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n   For \\(n=3\\) the obstruction disappears (the families \\(C,R,D\\) each have only three points) and an explicit configuration with three sunny lines exists (see §5).\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   The \\(n\\) vertical lines\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n   \\]\n   are all non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\) because each point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n5. **Construction for \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\ell_0 :\\; x=1 \\qquad\\text{(vertical, non‑sunny)},\\\\[2mm]\n   &\\ell_i :\\; y = x-(i-1)\\qquad\\text{for }i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   *Properties of the lines*  \n   *   For \\(i\\ge1\\) the slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n   *   The line \\(\\ell_0\\) is non‑sunny.\n\n   *Coverage proof*  \n   Take any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n   *If \\(a=1\\)*, the point lies on \\(\\ell_0\\).  \n\n   *If \\(a\\ge2\\)*, set \\(d=a-b\\). Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(a\\ge2\\), we have\n   \\[\n   0\\le d\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n   Choose \\(i=d+1\\) (so \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\)). Then \\(\\ell_i\\) has equation \\(y=x-d\\), and substituting \\((a,b)\\) gives \\(b=a-d\\), which holds by definition of \\(d\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_i\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{\\ell_0,\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\}\\), and exactly \\(n-1\\) of them are sunny.\n\n6. **Obtaining any smaller number of sunny lines.**  \n   Starting from the configuration of §5, replace arbitrarily any of the sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\;(i\\ge1)\\) by one of the three non‑sunny lines that also passes through its two defining points:\n\n   *   the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (already present);\n   *   the horizontal line \\(y=1\\);\n   *   the line of slope \\(-1\\) given by \\(x+y=n\\).\n\n   Each of these three lines contains the two points \\((1,i)\\) and \\((i,1)\\) that determine \\(\\ell_i\\); consequently the replacement does **not** destroy the covering of any point of \\(T_n\\).  \n   By performing the replacement on exactly \\(t\\) of the sunny lines we obtain a configuration with\n   \\[\n   k=(n-1)-t\\qquad (0\\le t\\le n-1)\n   \\]\n   sunny lines.  Hence every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable.\n\n7. **The case \\(n=3,\\;k=3\\).**  \n   The three lines\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\ell_1:\\; y = x ,\\\\\n   &\\ell_2:\\; \\text{through }(1,2)\\text{ and }(3,1),\\\\\n   &\\ell_3:\\; \\text{through }(2,1)\\text{ and }(1,3),\n   \\end{aligned}\n   