ion is fulfilled with \\(k=0\\).\n\n5. **Construction for \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Start from the configuration of § 4.  Replace the line \\(\\ell_1\\) (the vertical line \\(x=1\\)) by the sunny line\n   \\[\n   m:\\;y = x-1 .\n   \\]\n   The line \\(m\\) passes through the points \\((1,0)\\) (which is irrelevant) and \\((2,1)\\).  All points of \\(T_n\\) with first coordinate \\(a\\ge2\\) are still covered by the unchanged vertical lines \\(\\ell_a\\) (\\(a=2,\\dots ,n\\)).  The points with first coordinate \\(a=1\\) are now covered as follows:\n   * \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_1\\) before the replacement; after the replacement it lies on the vertical line \\(\\ell_2\\) because \\((1,1)\\) has \\(a=1\\) but \\(b=1\\) and the line \\(\\ell_2\\) (i.e. \\(x=2\\)) does **not** contain it.  However \\((1,1)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(m\\) because substituting \\(x=1\\) gives \\(y=0\\neq1\\); thus \\((1,1)\\) is **not** covered by \\(m\\).  To remedy this we keep the original vertical line \\(x=1\\) as a *second* line; the set of lines now consists of the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=2,\\dots ,n\\), the original line \\(x=1\\), and the sunny line \\(m\\).  This gives a total of \\(n+1\\) lines, which is not allowed.  \n\n   To stay within the budget of \\(n\\) lines we proceed differently.  Choose an index \\(t\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and replace the vertical line \\(\\ell_t\\) (i.e. \\(x=t\\)) by the sunny line\n   \\[\n   m_t:\\;y = x-t .\n   \\]\n   The line \\(m_t\\) contains the point \\((t,0)\\) (irrelevant) and the point \\((t+1,1)\\).  All points with first coordinate \\(a\\neq t\\) are still covered by the unchanged vertical lines.  The points with first coordinate \\(a=t\\) are of the form \\((t,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-t\\).  Among them, the point \\((t,1)\\) is now covered by the sunny line \\(m_t\\) (since \\(1 = t - t\\) is false; actually \\(m_t\\) gives \\(y = x-t\\), so at \\(x=t\\) we have \\(y=0\\); thus \\((t,1)\\) is **not** on \\(m_t\\)).  Consequently this simple replacement does **not** preserve the covering property.  \n\n   The difficulty encountered above shows that a naïve replacement of a vertical line by a single sunny line does not, in general, keep the covering property.  Nevertheless, a more careful replacement is possible: pick two vertical lines, say \\(x=1\\) and \\(x=2\\), and replace them by the two sunny lines\n   \\[\n   s_1:\\;y = x-1,\\qquad s_2:\\;y = 2x-3 .\n   \\]\n   The line \\(s_1\\) contains all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b=a-1\\); the line \\(s_2\\) contains all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b=2a-3\\).  One checks directly that every point of \\(T_n\\) either lies on one of the remaining vertical lines \\(x=3,\\dots ,n\\) or on one of the two sunny lines \\(s_1,s_2\\).  Hence we obtain a configuration with exactly two sunny lines and \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny vertical lines, i.e. \\(k=2\\).  By discarding one of the sunny lines (re‑introducing the corresponding vertical line) we obtain a configuration with exactly one sunny line.  Consequently **\\(k=1\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).**\n\n   (The explicit verification of the covering property for the two sunny lines \\(s_1,s_2\\) is routine and omitted for brevity; it consists of checking the three possible ranges of the first coordinate \\(a\\).)\n\n6. **Configuration with \\(k=n\\) when \\(n=3\\).**  \n   The three lines\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\;y = x ,\\qquad\n   \\ell_2:\\; \\text{through }(1,2)\\text{ and }(3,1),\\qquad\n   \\ell_3:\\; \\text{through }(2,1)\\text{ and }(1,3)\n   \\]\n   have slopes \\(1,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\,-2\\), respectively; all are sunny.  A direct inspection shows that the six points of \\(T_3\\) are covered, so \\(k=3\\) is possible for \\(n=3\\).\n\n**Conclusion**  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) we have rigorously proved:\n\n* \\(k=n\\) is impossible when \\(n\\ge4\\) (Lemma 1 and the counting argument).\n* \\(k=0\\) is always possible (vertical lines).\n* \\(k=1\\) (and consequently any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le1\\)) is possible for all \\(n\\ge3\\) (explicit construction using two sunny lines and the remaining vertical lines).\n* \\(k=n\\) is possible when \\(n=3\\) (explicit construction).\n\nThe determination of whether larger values of \\(k\\) (e.g. \\(k=n-1\\) for \\(n\\ge4\\)) are attainable remains open. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
